<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<introduction xml:id="daylight_intro">
        <p>
                In this group activity, you will use limited data to create theoretical models for the amount of daylight (measured from sunrise to sunset) in Grand Rapids, Michigan as a function of day of the year. Then, you will use a larger data set to create recursion models which you will compare and contrast with your models.
        </p>
        <p>
                        This activity is <alert>required</alert> for your portfolio. 
        </p>
        <p>
                        You are not being <em>assigned</em> specific roles in your groups, so your first order of business once you are in groups will be to negotiate and assign roles in your groups.
        </p>
        <p>
                Your final product for this group activity needs to contain:
                <ol>
                        <li>graphical representations of your data and your models;</li>
                        <li>a careful discussion of how you chose the parameters for your theoretical models;</li>
                        <li>a comparison of your parameters with those from your regression analysis;</li>
                        <li>a discussion of possible confounding issues.</li>
                </ol>
        </p>
        <p>
                        You also need to include a <alert>discussion of group roles</alert> and a summary of your group interactions, including how each person in your group contributed to the final result.
        </p>
        <p>
                You will have two entire class periods to work on this project.
        </p>
</introduction>
